Step‑by‑step: test TunOrient endpoints in Insomnia (Windows, exhaustive)

Prerequisites (on your machine)
1. Install VS Code, Python 3.8+ and Insomnia.
2. Open a terminal (PowerShell) and confirm python works:
```powershell
python --version
```

A. Start the API server (must run before using Insomnia)
1. Open PowerShell in project root: C:\Users\Administrateur\TunOrient
2. Create & activate virtual env (if you don’t have one):
```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```
(If activation blocked, run: Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Force)
3. Install requirements:
```powershell
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
4. Start the app (use uvicorn from venv):
```powershell
python -m uvicorn app.main:app --reload --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
```
5. Confirm server is up:
- Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs in browser → you should see the Swagger UI with /api/v1 endpoints.

B. Prepare Insomnia workspace + environment
1. Open Insomnia.
2. Create a new Workspace:
- Click “Create” → “New Workspace”.
- Name: TunOrient, Type: “Design Document” or “Request Collection” (choose Request Collection).
3. Create an Environment for the workspace:
- Click the Environment dropdown (top-left) → “Manage Environments”.
- Edit the Base Environment (or add one) and add variables:
  - base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1"
  - token = ""   (leave empty for now)
- Save and close.

C. Create requests (step‑by‑step)
1. Auth - Register (if your API supports):
- New Request → Name: Auth Register → POST → URL: {{ base_url }}/auth/register
- Header: Content-Type: application/json
- Body (JSON) — example (adjust to your schema):
```json
{
  "username": "student1@example.com",
  "password": "Password123",
  "role": "student"
}
```
- Click Send → expect 200/201 and response body with created user.

2. Auth - Login (get JWT)
- New Request → Name: Auth Login → POST → URL: {{ base_url }}/auth/login
- Header: Content-Type: application/json
- Body (JSON or form depending on your endpoint). Example JSON body:
```json
{
  "username": "student1@example.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}
```
- Send → response should contain token, e.g. { "access_token": "xxx", "token_type": "bearer" }.
- Copy the access_token value.

3. Store token in Insomnia environment (manual)
- Click Environment → Manage Environments → set token to the access_token string (no Bearer prefix) and save.
- Alternatively, set token to "Bearer <token>" if you prefer.

4. Create an Authorization header template for protected requests
- When creating other requests, add header:
  - Authorization: Bearer {{ token }}
(If you stored token including "Bearer ", then use {{ token }} directly.)

D. Example resource requests (use your schemas from /docs)
1. Create Student
- POST {{ base_url }}/students
- Header: Content-Type: application/json and Authorization: Bearer {{ token }}
- Body example:
```json
{
  "academic_level": "baccalaureate",
  "specialization": "Math",
  "region": "Tunis",
  "bac_average": 15.2
}
```
- Send → expect 201/200 and student object with id.

2. Get Student
- GET {{ base_url }}/students/{id}
- Replace {id} with returned student id (or set as path param in Insomnia).
- Authorization header required if endpoint protected.

3. List Programs
- GET {{ base_url }}/programs
- Auth header if needed
- Use query parameters in Insomnia if implemented (e.g., ?institution_id=2)

4. Create Admission Rule (admin/ministry)
- POST {{ base_url }}/admission-rules
- Body example:
```json
{
  "program_id": 1,
  "min_average": 12.5,
  "bac_type": "Math",
  "year": "2025"
}
```
- Protected endpoint — must use admin token (role enforcement depends on your app).

5. Run Recommendations (core test)
- POST {{ base_url }}/recommendations/{student_id}
- Replace {student_id} with student id
- Body: optional (e.g., {"limit": 10}) if your endpoint allows
- Send → Response: list of recommended programs and the API should also persist OrientationResult rows.

6. Get Recommendations (history)
- GET {{ base_url }}/recommendations/{student_id}
- Returns stored OrientationResult entries.

7. Ministerial Circulars
- POST /api/v1/circulars (admin)
- GET /api/v1/circulars (public)

E. Using Insomnia features to streamline tests
1. Environments: use {{ base_url }} and {{ token }} so you can switch hosts easily.
2. Chaining: after login, copy token manually into environment or use Insomnia plugin/response hook (advanced).
3. Request grouping: create folders in workspace for Auth, Students, Programs, Rules, Recommendations, Circulars.
4. Save example responses and use “Create Request from Response” for rapid scaffolding.

F. Verify DB state
1. Your SQLite file location (default from session.py): tunorient.db in project root:
   C:\Users\Administrateur\TunOrient\tunorient.db
2. Inspect with DB browser or sqlite3:
```powershell
# quick view using sqlite3 (if installed)
sqlite3 .\tunorient.db "SELECT * FROM students LIMIT 5;"
```

G. Troubleshooting common errors
- 401 Unauthorized: token missing or expired; ensure Authorization header = Bearer <token>.
- 404 Not Found: wrong path; verify exact path in http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs (watch /api/v1 prefix).
- 500 Server Error: check terminal where uvicorn runs for traceback and fix code.
- No tables / no data: ensure Base.metadata.create_all(bind=engine) is executed on startup or run migrations with Alembic.

H. Quick curl equivalents (useful for copying into Insomnia docs)
- Login:
```bash
curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/auth/login" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"username":"student1@example.com","password":"Password123"}'
```
- Create student (with token):
```bash
curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/students" -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"academic_level":"baccalaureate","specialization":"Math","region":"Tunis","bac_average":15.2}'
```

I. Final checklist before testing
- [ ] Server running at 127.0.0.1:8000
- [ ] /docs shows endpoints and request/response schemas
- [ ] Insomnia environment variables set (base_url, token)
- [ ] Authorization header added to protected requests
- [ ] SQLite DB file present (tunorient.db) for persistence checks

If you want, I can:
- export an Insomnia collection JSON with the example requests (Auth, Students, Programs, AdmissionRules, Recommendations, Circulars) that you can import directly — say “Export collection” and I will produce it.

