ons-said: okay now it works perfectky thanks I just have one problem with the logic of ministry users, what we mean here by ministry is the ministry of Higher Education and Scientific research, normally shouldnt it have only one account that the ministry logins with, its like we have allocated the ministry a uniaue account in our API that it uses to login and we must not allow multiple users with the role ministry it should be only one user

GitHub Copilot: GitHub Copilot

Short answer — yes. Enforce a single ministry account at user-creation and role-change time. Below are minimal, safe changes (application-level checks). Apply both patches and restart the server.

1) Enforce on user creation (auth service)
- Check for existing ministry user and raise an error if one exists.

````python
from sqlalchemy.orm import Session
from app.features.auth.model import User

class AuthService:
    def __init__(self, db: Session):
        self.db = db

    def create_user(self, email: str, hashed_password: str, role: str = "student"):
        # Prevent more than one ministry account (app-level guard)
        if role == "ministry":
            existing = self.db.query(User).filter(User.role == "ministry").first()
            if existing:
                raise ValueError("A ministry account already exists. Contact an admin to change it.")
        user = User(email=email, hashed_password=hashed_password, role=role)
        self.db.add(user)
        self.db.commit()
        self.db.refresh(user)
        return user

    def set_user_role(self, user_id: int, new_role: str):
        user = self.db.query(User).filter(User.id == user_id).first()
        if not user:
            raise ValueError("User not found")
        # Prevent promoting someone to ministry if one exists already
        if new_role == "ministry":
            existing = self.db.query(User).filter(User.role == "ministry").first()
            if existing and existing.id != user_id:
                raise ValueError("A ministry account already exists. Use transfer endpoint or contact admin.")
        user.role = new_role
        self.db.add(user)
        self.db.commit()
        self.db.refresh(user)
        return user
````

2) Use the service check in your register endpoint (auth router)
- Return a friendly 400 when creation is blocked.

````python
