ons-said: I just want to remind you that my system is not designed to be the official orientation system that students use to do their Orientation. My system is useful for students to get access to all resources that they need for their orientation process. Its meant to help them to get to know what are the different programs offered help them choose one that matches their interest thats why the recommendation system is a core module in the sytem. And this is an overview of my project problematic Academic orientation represents one of the most decisive phases in the educational journey of Tunisian students. Each year, thousands of high-school graduates face the challenge of selecting a university major that aligns with their academic performance, personal interests, and future employability. Despite the importance of this transition, the current orientation system in Tunisia remains constrained by limited access to structured information, centralized data, and personalized guidance.

In parallel, the rapid digital transformation of higher education and the labor market creates new opportunities for designing intelligent, data-driven tools that support students in making informed decisions. Modern web services and APIs provide a powerful framework to integrate university data, orientation rules, admission criteria, and personalized guidance in a unified system.

This project proposes the design and development of a web-based orientation service, delivered as a RESTful API, that centralizes and exposes relevant information to students, universities, and the Ministry of Higher Education.

\section{Problem Understanding}
\subsection{Context and Motivation}

Orientation and reorientation are critical stages in a student's academic journey, particularly in the Tunisian higher education system where choices made after secondary education strongly influence future career paths. Each year, thousands of students, ranging from first-year secondary students to baccalaureate graduates, face complex decisions regarding study fields, universities, and professional prospects.

In Tunisia, orientation decisions are mainly guided by official mechanisms provided by the Ministry of Higher Education, such as admission scores ("scores du dernier admis"), national orientation rules, and institutional program offerings. While these mechanisms ensure fairness and regulation, they often lack personalization and accessibility, especially for students who are still in pre-baccalaureate stages or those seeking reorientation.

\subsection{Identified Problems}

Despite the existence of official portals and guidance documents, several limitations persist:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Information related to universities, programs, admission requirements, and career outcomes is scattered across multiple platforms.
    \item Current systems primarily target baccalaureate graduates and provide limited support for younger students exploring potential study fields.
    \item Orientation processes are mostly rule-driven but not user-centric, offering little explanation or personalized guidance.
    \item There is no standardized Application Programming Interface (API) that centralizes orientation data and makes it reusable for developers, institutions, or educational platforms.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stakeholders}

The orientation ecosystem involves multiple stakeholders, each with specific needs:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Students}: baccalaureate graduates seeking admission information and recommendations, and pre-baccalaureate students exploring academic fields and career paths.
    \item \textbf{Universities and Higher Education Institutions}: providers of program data, admission conditions, and academic updates.
    \item \textbf{Ministry of Higher Education}: regulatory authority responsible for orientation rules, admission thresholds, and circular updates.
    \item \textbf{Third-party developers and platforms}: potential consumers of orientation data through a standardized API. so things like Choice list (min 6)	Yes	No	Enforce and manage choices
